ct over credit among the four members of its team.","confidence":0.86},"academicOrientation":{"score":62,"confidence":0.76},"profileConfidence":0.92,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"c88c8157195c5323acfa5ff8657f2f1d42f3035da1a0accb37b531bf5e5c1e07","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["central_figures_hit_schema_cap"]},"summary":"\"In a brilliant, definitive history of one of the most significant and controversial medical events of modern times, [the author] brings to light a bizarre clash of scientific personoalities. When F.G. Banting and J.J.R. Macleod won the 1923 Nobel Prize for discovering and isolating insulin, Banting immediately announed that he was dividing his share of the prize with his young associate, C.H. Best. Macleod divided his share with a fourth member of the team, J.B. Collip. For the next sixty years medical opinion was intensely divided over the allotment of credit for the discovery of insulin. In resolving this controversy, [the author] also offers a wealth of new detail on such subjects as the treatment of diabetes before insulin and the life-and-death struggle to manufacture insulin\"--Back cover.","displaySummary":"When F.G. Banting and J.J.R. Macleod won the 1923 Nobel Prize for discovering and isolating insulin, Banting immediately announed that he was dividing his share of the prize with his young associate, C.H. Best. Macleod divided his share with a fourth member of the team, J.B. Collip. For the next sixty years medical opinion was intensely divided over the allotment of credit for the discovery of insulin. In resolving this controversy, [the author] also offers a wealth of new detail on such subjects as the treatment of diabetes before insulin and the life-and-death struggle to manufacture insulin\"--Back cover.","primarySubject":"Medicine & Public Health","primaryTopic":"Drugs, Addiction & Treatment","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Medicine & Public Health","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence